[0.001, "o", "G\u001b[38;2;177;185;249muv\u001b[34G\u001b[39mand\u001b[38GPython\u001b[45G3.13\u001b[50Gare\u001b[54Galready\u001b[62Ginstalled,\u001b[73Gso\u001b[76GI'll\u001b[81Gmanage\u001b[88Gthe\u001b[92Gproject\u001b[100Gwith\u001b[105G\u001b[38;2;177;185;249muv\u001b[108G\u001b[39mand\u001b[112Gpin\u001b[116Git\u001b[119Gto\u001b[122G3.13.\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[1mNext question: how should amounts look in requests and responses?\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[22m\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B1.\u001b[6G\u001b[1mDecimal strings, e.g. \u001b[38;2;177;185;249m\"amount\": \"12.34\"\u001b[22m\u001b[39m: eas\u001b[52Gfor people\u001b[63Gto read and exact as t\u001b[86Gxt. The server ch\u001b[104Gcks there are at most 2 decimal place\u001b[142G, c\u001b[146Gnverts to whole cents\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B  \u001b[6Ginternally, and returns balances the same way (\u001b[38;2;177;185;249m\"-3.33\"\u001b[39m). Plai\u001b[68G JSON numbers like\u001b[87G\u001b[38;2;177;185;249m12.34\u001b[39m would be reject\u001b[109Gd, because they lose precision\u001b[140Gas\u001b[143Gfloating-point\u001b[158Gvalues.\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B2\u001b[6G\u001b[1mWhole cen\u001b[16Gs, e.g. \u001b[38;2;177;185;249m\"amount_cents\": 1234\u001b[22m\u001b[39m: the simplest option and fully exact, but clients\u001b[95Ghave to\u001b[103Gdo\u001b[106Gthe conversion themselves.\u001b[133G\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1"]
